{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And when you come.</b> After it mentioned the seed of the field and the seed of the woman, which is like the earth, it also mentioned that which is planted." "<b>And you shall plant.</b> Even an old tree. <b>And you shall plant</b> — this excludes layering, grafting, and that which sprouts on its own in the wilderness; but that which sprouts on its own in a settled area is obligated." "<b>Any tree of food.</b> This excludes barren trees, and one who plants for a fence, for beams, and for wood. From here one may rule regarding strawberries and blackberries and raspberries that grow as a fence for fields, vineyards, and grounds, that there is no orlah prohibition for them." "<b>Three years it shall be to you arelim.</b> It is not the way of the land to derive benefit from it until you bring from it an offering to HaShem, may He be blessed, for it is fitting to offer before Him the first of all produce. And during the first three years after its planting, it does not produce anything fine and acceptable to make an offering to HaShem, may He be blessed, because it has not yet taken root and become acclimated beforehand. <b>To you</b> — to bring the planting to the public."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "נרוזילי״ש and פרונילי״ש are Old French botanical terms in Hebrew letters; their identification as blackberries and raspberries is approximate."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:13:34.747593645Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}